1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,042
Somewhere in a chemical plant, a radar
level transmitter

2
00:00:04,042 --> 00:00:07,299
watches the surface of a liquid, twenty
meters below.

3
00:00:07,719 --> 00:00:11,563
It knows the full echo profile, its own
electronics temperature,

4
00:00:11,563 --> 00:00:14,567
the condition of its antenna, and weeks of
trends.

5
00:00:14,917 --> 00:00:16,559
And it reports — one number.

6
00:00:16,979 --> 00:00:20,286
A current between four and twenty
milliamps, on two wires.

7
00:00:20,706 --> 00:00:25,705
If you're lucky, a trickle of digital data
rides along at 1,200 bits per second

8
00:00:25,705 --> 00:00:28,237
— the speed of an early telephone modem.

9
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:30,901
Meanwhile, a few hundred meters away,

10
00:00:30,901 --> 00:00:35,939
the office network moves a billion bits
per second — almost a million times more.

11
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:40,831
This video is about the technology that
finally brings

12
00:00:40,831 --> 00:00:44,348
Ethernet all the way out to the
instrument: Ethernet-APL.

13
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,877
We'll take it apart down to the volts, the
symbols, and the joules.

14
00:00:49,297 --> 00:00:52,509
And as a reference point, we'll use a
network that many

15
00:00:52,509 --> 00:00:55,953
engineers already know from vehicles and
machines: CAN bus.

16
00:01:00,616 --> 00:01:04,456
Process instruments have spoken the same
basic language for decades.

17
00:01:04,876 --> 00:01:08,447
The 4–20 mA loop is brilliantly simple:
two wires,

18
00:01:08,447 --> 00:01:12,876
one analog value, and the same two wires
power the instrument.

19
00:01:13,296 --> 00:01:16,246
Four milliamps, not zero, means zero
percent

20
00:01:16,246 --> 00:01:18,928
— so a broken wire is instantly obvious.

21
00:01:19,982 --> 00:01:25,300
In the 1980s, HART added digital
communication by superimposing a small

22
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:30,393
tone on that current: 1,200 hertz for a
one, 2,200 hertz for a zero.

23
00:01:30,813 --> 00:01:35,173
Because the tone averages to zero, the
analog value is untouched

24
00:01:35,173 --> 00:01:38,578
— but the data rate is only 1,200 bits per
second.

25
00:01:39,632 --> 00:01:46,269
In the 1990s, fieldbuses such as
FOUNDATION Fieldbus H1 and PROFIBUS PA
went fully

26
00:01:46,269 --> 00:01:52,582
digital, at 31.25 kilobits per second —
shared by every device on the segment.

27
00:01:53,632 --> 00:01:56,496
Meanwhile, the rest of industry moved to
Ethernet,

28
00:01:56,496 --> 00:01:58,902
at a hundred megabits per second and more.

29
00:01:59,322 --> 00:02:02,052
On a logarithmic scale, the gap is
enormous.

30
00:02:03,116 --> 00:02:06,294
So why didn't plain Ethernet simply run
out into the plant?

31
00:02:06,714 --> 00:02:08,229
Four walls stood in the way.

32
00:02:08,582 --> 00:02:09,436
Distance.

33
00:02:09,856 --> 00:02:14,633
Standard 100BASE-TX Ethernet is limited to
one hundred meters per link,

34
00:02:14,633 --> 00:02:19,882
while field cables run for hundreds of
meters — sometimes a kilometer or more.

35
00:02:20,249 --> 00:02:21,081
Wires.

36
00:02:21,501 --> 00:02:23,939
Office Ethernet needs two or four pairs;

37
00:02:23,939 --> 00:02:26,621
plants are full of installed two-wire
cable.

38
00:02:26,982 --> 00:02:27,729
Power.

39
00:02:28,149 --> 00:02:30,666
A field instrument has no local supply.

40
00:02:31,086 --> 00:02:34,628
It must be powered over the same two wires
that carry its signal.

41
00:02:34,982 --> 00:02:36,454
And explosion protection.

42
00:02:36,874 --> 00:02:40,438
In hazardous areas, the energy in a
circuit must be limited,

43
00:02:40,438 --> 00:02:43,466
so that not even a fault can ignite the
atmosphere.

44
00:02:43,886 --> 00:02:46,830
Ordinary Ethernet was never designed for
that.

45
00:02:47,682 --> 00:02:53,100
Ethernet-APL — the Advanced Physical Layer
— was designed to break all four walls.

46
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:55,056
The key word is “physical”.

47
00:02:58,715 --> 00:03:02,404
APL replaces only the bottom layer of the
network stack:

48
00:03:02,404 --> 00:03:06,224
the copper, the voltages, the power, and
the safety rules.

49
00:03:06,644 --> 00:03:12,861
Everything above it is unchanged Ethernet:
the same frames and MAC addresses,

50
00:03:12,861 --> 00:03:17,060
IP, TCP and UDP, and the industrial
protocols on top

51
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:21,259
— PROFINET, EtherNet/IP, HART-IP, OPC UA,
Modbus TCP

52
00:03:21,259 --> 00:03:24,650
— even a web server inside the instrument.

53
00:03:25,615 --> 00:03:32,302
Its foundation is 10BASE-T1L, part of the
IEEE 802.3cg standard from 2019: ten

54
00:03:32,302 --> 00:03:39,503
megabits per second, full duplex, over a
single twisted pair, for up to a
kilometer.

55
00:03:39,923 --> 00:03:45,590
On top of that, the APL project — a joint
effort of FieldComm Group, ODVA,

56
00:03:45,590 --> 00:03:50,109
the OPC Foundation, and PROFIBUS and
PROFINET International

57
00:03:50,109 --> 00:03:52,636
— added what process plants need:

58
00:03:52,636 --> 00:03:57,844
port profiles that define how power is
delivered over the same pair,

59
00:03:57,844 --> 00:04:00,065
standardized as IEC TS 63444,

60
00:04:00,065 --> 00:04:05,886
and an intrinsic-safety concept called
2-WISE, published as IEC TS 60079-47.

61
00:04:06,848 --> 00:04:11,189
The result: two wires, ten megabits per
second in both directions,

62
00:04:11,189 --> 00:04:12,767
power and data together,

63
00:04:12,767 --> 00:04:14,543
trunks up to one kilometer,

64
00:04:14,543 --> 00:04:18,752
and intrinsically safe spurs that reach
all the way into Zone 0.

65
00:04:23,415 --> 00:04:26,487
Before we open up APL, let's set up our
reference.

66
00:04:26,907 --> 00:04:29,361
CAN, the Controller Area Network,

67
00:04:29,361 --> 00:04:35,014
was presented by Bosch in 1986 to tame the
growing tangle of wiring in cars.

68
00:04:35,434 --> 00:04:39,807
Today it's everywhere: vehicles, tractors,
robots, medical devices.

69
00:04:40,765 --> 00:04:42,957
Physically, CAN is one twisted pair,

70
00:04:42,957 --> 00:04:46,184
called CAN high and CAN low, running past
every node.

71
00:04:46,604 --> 00:04:50,110
A 120-ohm resistor at each end matches the
cable's

72
00:04:50,110 --> 00:04:53,686
characteristic impedance, so signals don't
reflect.

73
00:04:54,648 --> 00:04:56,056
CAN has two bus states.

74
00:04:56,476 --> 00:05:00,853
In the recessive state, nobody drives the
bus: the termination resistors

75
00:05:00,853 --> 00:05:05,351
pull both wires to about two and a half
volts, and the difference is zero.

76
00:05:05,771 --> 00:05:10,643
To send a dominant bit, a transceiver
pushes CAN high up to about three and a
half

77
00:05:10,643 --> 00:05:15,456
volts and pulls CAN low down to one and a
half — a difference of about two volts.

78
00:05:15,815 --> 00:05:17,884
The receiver looks only at the difference.

79
00:05:18,304 --> 00:05:22,926
It's exactly like a differential-pressure
transmitter, which ignores the static

80
00:05:22,926 --> 00:05:27,840
line pressure: interference that couples
equally into both wires simply cancels
out.

81
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:31,332
APL, as we'll see, uses the very same
trick.

82
00:05:32,798 --> 00:05:34,270
Now the crucial property.

83
00:05:34,690 --> 00:05:36,248
Dominant always wins.

84
00:05:36,668 --> 00:05:40,451
If even one node drives a dominant zero,
the bus is dominant,

85
00:05:40,451 --> 00:05:43,366
no matter how many others send a recessive
one.

86
00:05:43,786 --> 00:05:48,480
Engineers call this a wired-AND, and CAN
builds its entire access method on it.

87
00:05:49,432 --> 00:05:52,504
Suppose three nodes start a frame at the
same instant.

88
00:05:52,924 --> 00:05:57,958
Each one sends its eleven-bit identifier,
bit by bit, while listening to the bus.

89
00:05:58,378 --> 00:06:02,214
At the fifth bit, the cabin-temperature
node sends a recessive one,

90
00:06:02,214 --> 00:06:03,989
but reads back a dominant zero.

91
00:06:04,409 --> 00:06:07,269
Someone with a more important message is
talking,

92
00:06:07,269 --> 00:06:09,721
so it stops immediately, and just listens.

93
00:06:10,141 --> 00:06:13,768
Three bits later, the engine-speed node
loses the same way.

94
00:06:14,188 --> 00:06:17,598
The brake-pressure message, with the
lowest identifier,

95
00:06:17,598 --> 00:06:20,140
wins — and its frame continues undamaged.

96
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:23,824
No collision, no retransmission, no time
lost.

97
00:06:24,244 --> 00:06:28,468
This is bitwise arbitration, and it's why
CAN is so deterministic:

98
00:06:28,468 --> 00:06:31,860
the most important message always gets
through first.

99
00:06:32,815 --> 00:06:36,374
A classic CAN frame carries at most eight
bytes of data,

100
00:06:36,374 --> 00:06:38,980
wrapped in nearly fifty bits of overhead:

101
00:06:38,980 --> 00:06:42,031
the identifier, control bits, a fifteen-
bit CRC,

102
00:06:42,031 --> 00:06:45,082
an acknowledge slot, and an end-of-frame
marker.

103
00:06:45,448 --> 00:06:50,060
After five identical bits, the transmitter
inserts one opposite stuff bit,

104
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:52,552
so the receivers never lose their clock.

105
00:06:53,515 --> 00:06:56,566
Notice what's missing: there's no
destination address.

106
00:06:56,986 --> 00:07:00,387
The identifier names the content, like
“engine speed”,

107
00:07:00,387 --> 00:07:03,599
and every node decides for itself whether
it cares.

108
00:07:04,965 --> 00:07:08,186
But arbitration has a price, and it is
paid in meters.

109
00:07:08,606 --> 00:07:10,117
For the wired-AND to work,

110
00:07:10,117 --> 00:07:13,834
a dominant bit from the far end of the bus
must reach every node

111
00:07:13,834 --> 00:07:18,249
— and the transmitter must read back the
result — before the bit is sampled.

112
00:07:18,615 --> 00:07:21,452
Signals travel about five nanoseconds per
meter.

113
00:07:21,872 --> 00:07:24,350
So the round trip to the far end and back,

114
00:07:24,350 --> 00:07:28,422
plus the transceiver delays, has to fit
inside a fraction of one bit.

115
00:07:28,842 --> 00:07:32,731
At one megabit per second, a bit lasts
just one microsecond,

116
00:07:32,731 --> 00:07:35,519
which limits the bus to about forty
meters.

117
00:07:35,882 --> 00:07:37,311
Want two hundred meters?

118
00:07:37,731 --> 00:07:40,654
Then slow down, so that every bit lasts
long enough.

119
00:07:41,615 --> 00:07:44,324
Plot the usual limits on logarithmic axes.

120
00:07:44,744 --> 00:07:46,088
Five hundred meters?

121
00:07:46,508 --> 00:07:48,812
125 kilobits per second.

122
00:07:49,232 --> 00:07:50,171
A kilometer?

123
00:07:50,591 --> 00:07:51,594
About fifty.

124
00:07:52,014 --> 00:07:56,408
Bit rate times length is roughly constant:
CAN trades speed for distance.

125
00:07:56,765 --> 00:08:00,285
That's also why CAN FD only accelerates
the data phase.

126
00:08:00,705 --> 00:08:03,971
Once arbitration is over and a single node
is talking,

127
00:08:03,971 --> 00:08:06,209
the round-trip constraint disappears.

128
00:08:10,465 --> 00:08:13,686
Now let's look at Ethernet-APL with the
same eyes.

129
00:08:14,106 --> 00:08:15,408
First, topology.

130
00:08:15,828 --> 00:08:17,214
There is no shared bus.

131
00:08:17,634 --> 00:08:19,922
Every link connects exactly two ports,

132
00:08:19,922 --> 00:08:23,053
point to point, and switches tie the links
together.

133
00:08:24,015 --> 00:08:26,831
A typical plant uses a trunk-and-spur
layout.

134
00:08:27,251 --> 00:08:30,374
In the safe area, an APL power switch
connects

135
00:08:30,374 --> 00:08:33,565
to the plant network and feeds a trunk
cable up

136
00:08:33,565 --> 00:08:37,095
to one kilometer long, carrying both data
and power,

137
00:08:37,095 --> 00:08:39,539
with a 2.4-volt peak-to-peak signal.

138
00:08:39,898 --> 00:08:43,369
Out in the hazardous area, a field switch
takes that power

139
00:08:43,369 --> 00:08:46,960
and distributes it over spurs up to two
hundred meters long.

140
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:49,870
Each spur serves exactly one instrument,

141
00:08:49,870 --> 00:08:53,481
with a lower one-volt signal and
intrinsically safe power.

142
00:08:53,832 --> 00:08:57,508
And because every spur is its own link,
every instrument gets

143
00:08:57,508 --> 00:09:01,426
its own ten megabits per second, in each
direction, all the time.

144
00:09:01,846 --> 00:09:04,577
Nothing is shared, so there's nothing to
arbitrate.

145
00:09:06,332 --> 00:09:09,553
Wait — both directions at the same time,
on one pair?

146
00:09:09,973 --> 00:09:13,024
In CAN, two transmitters driving the bus
at once

147
00:09:13,024 --> 00:09:16,202
is exactly the conflict that arbitration
resolves.

148
00:09:16,622 --> 00:09:18,756
In APL, it's the normal state.

149
00:09:19,115 --> 00:09:23,872
Both ends transmit continuously, and on
the wire the two signals simply add up.

150
00:09:24,292 --> 00:09:29,320
So each receiver sees the far-end signal —
weakened by up to about 25 dB

151
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:34,767
after a kilometer of cable — buried under
its own, much stronger transmission.

152
00:09:35,132 --> 00:09:38,758
The trick is that every transceiver knows
exactly what it just sent.

153
00:09:39,178 --> 00:09:43,889
An adaptive digital filter — the echo
canceller — learns how its own signal

154
00:09:43,889 --> 00:09:48,224
appears at its input, including
reflections, and subtracts that copy.

155
00:09:48,644 --> 00:09:50,329
What remains is the far end.

156
00:09:50,749 --> 00:09:53,864
It's the same principle as noise-
cancelling headphones.

157
00:09:54,915 --> 00:09:58,456
So here is the deepest difference between
the two physical layers.

158
00:09:58,876 --> 00:10:02,688
CAN uses a nonlinear medium, where
dominant beats recessive,

159
00:10:02,688 --> 00:10:04,530
so that nodes can take turns.

160
00:10:04,950 --> 00:10:10,582
APL uses a linear medium plus signal
processing, so that both ends can talk at
once.

161
00:10:12,248 --> 00:10:15,651
Next: how do you push ten megabits per
second through a

162
00:10:15,651 --> 00:10:19,672
kilometer of cable originally designed for
slow fieldbus signals?

163
00:10:20,092 --> 00:10:21,649
You keep the frequencies low.

164
00:10:22,515 --> 00:10:25,544
CAN uses two voltage levels: one bit per
symbol.

165
00:10:25,964 --> 00:10:29,804
10BASE-T1L uses three: plus, zero, and
minus.

166
00:10:30,224 --> 00:10:31,867
This is called PAM-3.

167
00:10:32,831 --> 00:10:37,631
The coder takes four data bits at a time
and maps them onto three ternary symbols.

168
00:10:38,051 --> 00:10:42,467
Four bits have sixteen combinations; three
ternary symbols have twenty-seven.

169
00:10:42,887 --> 00:10:46,127
The spare codes let the encoder choose, at
every step,

170
00:10:46,127 --> 00:10:49,186
the pattern that keeps the average voltage
at zero,

171
00:10:49,186 --> 00:10:53,085
so there's no DC drift — which matters, as
we'll see in a moment.

172
00:10:54,048 --> 00:10:56,757
Symbols are sent at 7.5 megabaud.

173
00:10:57,177 --> 00:10:59,890
Seven and a half million symbols per
second,

174
00:10:59,890 --> 00:11:04,452
times four-thirds of a bit per symbol, is
exactly ten megabits per second.

175
00:11:05,415 --> 00:11:09,959
Because the data is scrambled first, the
spectrum stays smooth, whatever you send.

176
00:11:10,379 --> 00:11:16,172
Most of the energy sits below the Nyquist
frequency of 3.75 megahertz, where a

177
00:11:16,172 --> 00:11:22,261
kilometer of cable costs about 25 dB — a
loss the receiver's equalizer can handle.

178
00:11:23,915 --> 00:11:27,947
Power travels on the same two wires, and
the trick is frequency, again.

179
00:11:28,367 --> 00:11:30,969
Power is direct current, at zero hertz.

180
00:11:31,389 --> 00:11:35,881
The data lives between roughly a hundred
kilohertz and a few megahertz

181
00:11:35,881 --> 00:11:39,987
— and, thanks to the DC-free code, it
contains no DC of its own.

182
00:11:40,948 --> 00:11:42,675
So at each end of the cable,

183
00:11:42,675 --> 00:11:46,560
inductors pass the direct current to and
from the power supply,

184
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:49,397
but block the fast data, while capacitors
pass

185
00:11:49,397 --> 00:11:52,233
the data to the transceiver, but block the
DC.

186
00:11:52,653 --> 00:11:56,971
A mechanical engineer would recognize it
immediately: a static load

187
00:11:56,971 --> 00:12:02,061
with a vibration riding on top, separated
by a low-pass and a high-pass filter.

188
00:12:03,015 --> 00:12:04,060
How much power?

189
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:07,106
An APL power switch can feed a trunk with
up

190
00:12:07,106 --> 00:12:09,493
to about ninety-two watts at the source.

191
00:12:09,913 --> 00:12:13,215
An instrument on an intrinsically safe
spur gets roughly

192
00:12:13,215 --> 00:12:16,399
half a watt to one watt, depending on its
power class.

193
00:12:16,819 --> 00:12:21,057
That sounds modest — but it's more than
ten times the forty milliwatts or

194
00:12:21,057 --> 00:12:24,541
so that a four-to-twenty milliamp
instrument has to live on.

195
00:12:28,898 --> 00:12:31,650
Now the fourth wall: explosive
atmospheres.

196
00:12:32,070 --> 00:12:36,529
An explosion needs three things: fuel,
oxygen, and an ignition source.

197
00:12:36,949 --> 00:12:40,149
In a process plant, you can't always
remove the first two.

198
00:12:41,315 --> 00:12:43,299
Areas are classified into zones.

199
00:12:43,719 --> 00:12:48,884
Zone 0: an explosive atmosphere is present
continuously or for long periods

200
00:12:48,884 --> 00:12:50,951
— inside a tank, for instance.

201
00:12:51,371 --> 00:12:53,931
Zone 1: likely in normal operation.

202
00:12:54,351 --> 00:12:57,807
Zone 2: not expected, and only briefly if
it happens.

203
00:12:58,765 --> 00:13:01,538
Intrinsic safety removes the ignition
source.

204
00:13:01,958 --> 00:13:06,380
Voltage, current and power are limited —
and so is the energy stored

205
00:13:06,380 --> 00:13:10,606
in the circuit's capacitance and
inductance, one half C V squared

206
00:13:10,606 --> 00:13:15,028
and one half L I squared — so that even
the spark from a broken wire

207
00:13:15,028 --> 00:13:19,579
or a short circuit stays below the minimum
ignition energy of the gas.

208
00:13:19,999 --> 00:13:23,029
For hydrogen, that's only about twenty
microjoules.

209
00:13:24,081 --> 00:13:26,651
Protection level “i-a” means the circuit

210
00:13:26,651 --> 00:13:29,543
stays safe even with two simultaneous
faults.

211
00:13:29,963 --> 00:13:32,160
That's what's required to enter Zone 0.

212
00:13:33,115 --> 00:13:37,160
Traditionally, every intrinsically safe
loop needed a calculation:

213
00:13:37,160 --> 00:13:38,753
does the source's voltage,

214
00:13:38,753 --> 00:13:40,776
current and power fit the device,

215
00:13:40,776 --> 00:13:44,699
and does the cable's capacitance and
inductance fit what's left?

216
00:13:45,119 --> 00:13:48,276
2-WISE replaces that with predefined port
profiles.

217
00:13:48,696 --> 00:13:53,180
A 2-WISE field-switch port and a 2-WISE
instrument with matching profiles

218
00:13:53,180 --> 00:13:57,848
are designed to be safe together, on up to
two hundred meters of spur cable.

219
00:13:58,268 --> 00:14:02,364
You compare the labels, and you're done —
much like FISCO did for fieldbus.

220
00:14:02,715 --> 00:14:05,800
CAN has no standardized plug-and-play
equivalent:

221
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:10,459
intrinsically safe CAN systems exist, but
they're engineered case by case.

222
00:14:10,879 --> 00:14:14,719
For APL, intrinsic safety was a design
goal from day one.

223
00:14:19,081 --> 00:14:20,873
Let's put the two side by side.

224
00:14:21,293 --> 00:14:22,168
Bandwidth.

225
00:14:22,588 --> 00:14:25,566
On classic CAN, one megabit per second at
most —

226
00:14:25,566 --> 00:14:29,351
and that's at forty meters — shared by
every node on the bus.

227
00:14:29,771 --> 00:14:32,544
With ten nodes, each gets, on average, a
tenth.

228
00:14:32,964 --> 00:14:38,297
On APL, every device has a private ten
megabits per second, in each direction.

229
00:14:39,248 --> 00:14:40,101
Distance.

230
00:14:40,521 --> 00:14:43,273
Back to our chart of bit rate versus cable
length.

231
00:14:43,693 --> 00:14:47,277
CAN falls along that diagonal: double the
length, halve the speed.

232
00:14:47,697 --> 00:14:53,009
10BASE-T1L stays flat at ten megabits per
second, all the way to a kilometer.

233
00:14:53,429 --> 00:14:56,629
Its speed-distance product is about two
hundred times higher.

234
00:14:57,581 --> 00:14:58,413
Frames.

235
00:14:58,833 --> 00:15:02,183
A CAN frame carries up to eight bytes, and
names its content.

236
00:15:02,603 --> 00:15:05,927
An Ethernet frame carries up to fifteen
hundred bytes,

237
00:15:05,927 --> 00:15:09,560
and names its destination and its source
with MAC addresses

238
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:14,485
— while IP addresses let it be routed
across the whole plant network, or beyond.

239
00:15:15,448 --> 00:15:16,941
What does that mean in practice?

240
00:15:17,361 --> 00:15:21,393
Take one megabyte — say, a firmware
update, or a diagnostic log.

241
00:15:21,813 --> 00:15:23,669
Over HART: almost two hours.

242
00:15:24,089 --> 00:15:26,265
Over a fieldbus: about four minutes.

243
00:15:26,685 --> 00:15:30,355
Over CAN at one megabit per second: around
fifteen seconds.

244
00:15:30,775 --> 00:15:33,505
Over Ethernet-APL: less than a second.

245
00:15:34,465 --> 00:15:38,497
Power, and hazardous areas: CAN leaves
both to the system designer.

246
00:15:38,917 --> 00:15:44,122
APL delivers power over the data pair, and
it is intrinsically safe by design.

247
00:15:46,581 --> 00:15:48,352
So is APL simply better?

248
00:15:48,772 --> 00:15:49,433
No.

249
00:15:49,853 --> 00:15:54,336
CAN is cheap: a CAN controller is built
into countless microcontrollers,

250
00:15:54,336 --> 00:15:56,701
and its frames are tiny and efficient.

251
00:15:57,121 --> 00:16:01,333
Its arbitration gives hard, priority-based
timing on short buses

252
00:16:01,333 --> 00:16:04,097
— exactly what vehicles and machines need.

253
00:16:04,448 --> 00:16:07,176
Ethernet-APL solves a different problem:

254
00:16:07,176 --> 00:16:10,654
long cables, hazardous areas, power over
two wires,

255
00:16:10,654 --> 00:16:13,178
and rich data from every instrument —

256
00:16:13,178 --> 00:16:17,611
using the same IP-based networking as the
rest of the enterprise.

257
00:16:19,465 --> 00:16:20,574
So, to sum up.

258
00:16:20,994 --> 00:16:22,253
CAN is a party line.

259
00:16:22,673 --> 00:16:26,909
Everyone shares one pair of wires, the
most important voice always wins,

260
00:16:26,909 --> 00:16:29,969
and speed and distance trade off against
each other.

261
00:16:30,331 --> 00:16:34,006
Ethernet-APL is a private line from every
instrument:

262
00:16:34,006 --> 00:16:37,819
point to point, full duplex, ten megabits
per second up

263
00:16:37,819 --> 00:16:41,494
to a kilometer, power and data on the same
two wires,

264
00:16:41,494 --> 00:16:44,753
and intrinsically safe all the way into
Zone 0.

265
00:16:45,715 --> 00:16:47,101
The four walls are down.

266
00:16:47,521 --> 00:16:52,337
After decades of four to twenty milliamps,
the process plant can finally speak the

267
00:16:52,337 --> 00:16:54,804
same language as the rest of the network —

268
00:16:54,804 --> 00:16:57,271
from the sensor, all the way to the cloud.

269
00:16:59,281 --> 00:17:00,519
Thanks for watching.
